---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import ToolVote from '../../../components/ToolVote.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cambium"
  description="Most architecture tools draw what an organization has. Cambium draws what it costs to get something through it — and then helps you settle the things that make it cost. Working prototype."
>
  <main class="reading" id="main">
    <section class="block insight-page">
      <p class="kicker"><a href="/tools/">Tools</a></p>
      <h1>Cambium</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Most architecture tools draw what an organization has: systems, teams, boxes, lines. Cambium draws
        what it costs to get something through it — and then helps you settle the things that make it cost.
        It is a model of an organization in motion rather than a picture of one at rest.
      </p>

      <h2>The name</h2>

      <p>
        The cambium is the thin living layer just under a tree's bark. It carries sap from the roots to the
        canopy, and it is the layer a girdling cut severs. Cut it and the tree stays green from the road for
        weeks while it dies standing up. Circulation is the part that matters and the part you cannot see from
        outside — which is a fair description of most organizations too.
      </p>

      <h2>What it does</h2>

      <p>
        You model an organization once — what it must always be able to do, who does it, what it runs on —
        and the tool reads that model for the places work will get stuck. Nothing is typed in by hand; it is already implied by
        the model, and a tool that knows the vocabulary can say so out loud.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the first half. A list of what is wrong can only ever repeat itself, so the second half is
        about closing them.
      </p>

      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>
          <strong>It finds.</strong> Capabilities with no owner, one with two claimed owners, work owned by
          one team and built by another, a role resting on a single person, a word four systems each hold
          their own version of. Each names the people who disagree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It offers options, never recommendations.</strong> Open a finding and you get the available
          moves with what each buys and what each costs, side by side. Every one costs something, and which
          trade is right depends on things the map does not hold.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It settles.</strong> An agreement is about one thing and needs the parties to sign it. A
          guideline is a rule about a class of things, signed once and then checked against everything new —
          the signature that scales, because two hundred people each negotiating three agreements is six
          hundred signatures, and a control that expensive is one somebody eventually removes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A decision applies itself.</strong> Recording one does not only write it down: the rule it
          announces becomes a guideline, the meaning it settles becomes an agreement, the competence it
          requires attaches to the capability that needs it, the address it lands on is written on the thing.
          Everything it creates arrives unsigned. A decision is somebody choosing; a rule is the organization
          agreeing to be held to it, and the distance between those two is usually larger than anybody
          expects.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No score.</strong> Nobody has measured enough organizations for a number to mean anything,
          and a number invites comparison with other people instead of with your own reading of the same place
          in six months. The four indicators are reported separately and never summed — a single
          organizational number acquires a target, a target acquires a bonus, and a measure with a bonus
          attached stops measuring anything.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why it changed</h2>

      <p>
        An AI agent that writes code can produce more in an afternoon than a team can decide about in a month.
        The constraint stops being competence and becomes <em>decidedness</em> — what the organization has
        actually settled, in writing, that work can proceed from. Which is the thing this tool was already
        measuring.
      </p>

      <p>
        So agents are on the map beside the people. They hold competence, sit in teams, work in repositories,
        and count toward the load. The one thing they may not do is sign: a signature records that somebody
        would stand behind a sentence afterwards, in front of the people it lands on, and that cannot be
        delegated to something a vendor can switch off on a Tuesday.
      </p>

      <p>
        What an agent may do is never a setting. It comes from the rules in force where it is working — act
        where a signed rule already decided the matter, prepare and stop where a judgement remains, ask where
        no rule reaches. There is deliberately no switch that grants autonomy. An organization whose agents
        ask less is one that has decided more things out loud, and the tool turns each answer into a rule so
        the same question is not asked twice.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes out</h2>

      <p>
        Guardrails in prose for whoever is about to do the work, ordered so somebody skimming can stop after
        the first section and still not do anything they would have to undo. A machine-readable policy for an
        agent platform to gate on — deny by default, a reason on every verdict, a fingerprint so it can tell
        when it is holding a stale one. A pre-commit hook that blocks exactly one thing: breaking a signed
        rule without saying why.
      </p>

      <p>
        The model is YAML in git rather than a database. Diffs, history for free, code review on
        organizational change, and a map that survives this tool being replaced — which is the last thing it
        asserts: a model is only worth having if the organization it describes can argue with it in the open,
        one commit at a time.
      </p>

      <h2>The metamodel</h2>

      <p>
        Twenty-two entity types in four bands: what is durable, how the place is arranged this decade, what it
        currently runs on — and the band most models leave out, which records how a decision was made, who may
        act on it without asking, and what anybody actually went and measured. The first three can be read off
        things an organization already keeps. The fourth cannot, and it is the one that makes the others tell
        you how fast the organization moves rather than only what it contains.
      </p>

      <p>
        Small enough to hold in your head, because a notation you can hold in your head is one people keep
        current, and a model nobody updates is worse than no model at all.
      </p>

      <p class="demo-open">
        <a class="demo-button" href="/tools/cambium-metamodel.html">Read the metamodel →</a>
        <span class="demo-hint">First iteration. Twenty-two entities, six rules.</span>
      </p>

      <h2>The mock-up</h2>

      <p>
        Below is a snapshot to look around in. The button opens the prototype, where things can be changed —
        and it opens on <em>what supports what</em>: capabilities as boxes, with a team, system or person that
        belongs to only one sitting inside it, and anything supporting several sitting outside them all,
        ringed. Switch the layers, switch the diagram, or
        <a href="/tools/cambium-app.html?view=graph&amp;full=1&amp;diagram=what-supports-what">open it full-screen</a>. The rest of what the tool does is in there rather than
        described here.
      </p>

      <p class="demo-open">
        <a class="demo-button" href="/tools/cambium-app.html?view=graph&amp;full=1&amp;diagram=what-supports-what">Open the clickable prototype →</a>
        <span class="demo-hint">It is an application. A big screen suits it.</span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="demo-wide">
      <iframe
        id="cambium-demo"
        src="/tools/cambium-snapshot.html"
        title="Cambium mock-up"
        loading="lazy"
      ></iframe>
      <figure class="demo-narrow">
        <img
          src="/tools/cambium-demo.gif"
          alt="The Cambium canvas: capability cards on the left, computed ownership findings on the right."
          loading="lazy"
          width="1146"
          height="941"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The mock-up is an application and wants a wider screen than this one, so here it is as a recording.
          The real thing is one tap away, and worth a bigger screen.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="demo-fallback">
        <a href="/tools/cambium-app.html?view=graph&amp;full=1&amp;diagram=what-supports-what">Open the mock-up full-screen</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="block insight-page">
      <h2>Where it is</h2>

      <p>
        A prototype on my own machine, running against organizations I made up to carry every problem the tool
        is meant to catch. No date, no beta, no waiting list. If you work somewhere this would have to
        survive, <a href="/contact/">write to me</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="tool-foot">
        The vocabulary behind it — capability, structural friction, the interface ladder — comes from
        <a href="/organizational-flow/">Organizational Flow</a>.
      </p>

      <ToolVote tool="cambium" />
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .insight-page h2 {
    font-family: 'Fraunces', serif;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    margin: 2.6rem 0 0.7rem;
  }
  .feature-list {
    margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .feature-list li {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .feature-list strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  /* The mock-up is an application rather than a page: it wants a viewport, so it
     steps out of the reading column and keeps a fixed one. */
  .demo-wide {
    width: min(65rem, calc(100vw - 3rem));
    margin: 2rem 0 3.4rem 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .demo-wide {
      width: 100vw;
    }
  }
  /* The mock-up does not reflow below about 700px — it is a desktop tool, and
     pretending otherwise would just clip it. Phones get the recording instead. */
  .demo-narrow {
    display: none;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .demo-narrow img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--mist);
  }
  .demo-narrow figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    padding: 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--bark);
  }
  .demo-wide iframe {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: min(46rem, 82vh);
    border: 1px solid var(--mist);
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--card);
  }
  .demo-open {
    margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
  }
  .demo-hint {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 0.8rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--bark);
  }
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .demo-hint {
      display: block;
      margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    }
  }
  .demo-button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--moss);
    border-radius: 2px;
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--moss);
    background: none;
    transition: background 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
  }
  .demo-button:hover {
    background: var(--moss);
    color: var(--paper);
  }
  .demo-fallback {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .demo-fallback {
      padding: 0 1.5rem;
    }
  }
  /* Last, so it wins over the base display rules above. */
  @media (max-width: 44rem) {
    .demo-wide iframe {
      display: none;
    }
    .demo-narrow {
      display: block;
    }
    .demo-fallback {
      padding: 0 1.5rem;
    }
  }
  .tool-foot {
    margin-top: 2.8rem;
    padding-top: 1.3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--mist);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--bark);
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Same-origin, so the site's theme toggle can be handed through to the frame.
  // The height is fixed here: the mock-up is a full-viewport application and
  // measuring its body would just report the frame back to itself.
  const frame = document.getElementById('cambium-demo') as HTMLIFrameElement | null;

  if (frame) {
    const syncTheme = () => {
      const doc = frame.contentDocument;
      if (!doc) return;
      const theme = document.documentElement.dataset.theme;
      if (theme) doc.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;
      else delete doc.documentElement.dataset.theme;
    };

    frame.addEventListener('load', syncTheme);
    if (frame.contentDocument?.readyState === 'complete') syncTheme();

    new MutationObserver(syncTheme).observe(document.documentElement, {
      attributes: true,
      attributeFilter: ['data-theme'],
    });
  }
</script>
